<form class="totalform help-on-hover help-box" data-method="POST" data-route="/admin/settings/oauth"><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: enabled;"><label for="field-UID">Enable OAuth Server</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="enabled" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID" checked/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Enable OAuth Server</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Master switch for the OAuth 2.1 authorization server. When off, the OAuth discovery documents and endpoints return 404, so MCP clients that see a discoverable OAuth server (Claude, Claude Desktop) connect anonymously to the public tier instead of demanding a login. Turn off to run a public-only MCP site — access is then anonymous (public collections) or API key (admin). Existing access tokens expire naturally (default 1 hour) and cannot be refreshed.</p></div><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: dynamicRegistration;"><label for="field-UID">Allow Dynamic Registration</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="dynamicRegistration" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID" checked/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Allow Dynamic Registration</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Enable RFC 7591 self-registration at <code>POST /oauth/register</code>. On by default — MCP clients (Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor) need it to connect without manual setup. Self-registration alone never grants data access: a registered client still needs an admin to approve its consent screen, which marks the client as self-registered and shows where approval sends you. Registrations are rate-limited and unused ones are pruned after a day. Turn off to require an admin-created static client for every integration.</p></div><div class="form-field select-field " data-type="select" style="--grid-area: accessTokenTtl;"><label for="field-UID">Access Token Lifetime</label><div class="form-group"><select id="field-UID" name="accessTokenTtl" aria-describedby="help-UID"><option value="PT15M">15 minutes</option><option value="PT30M">30 minutes</option><option value="PT1H" selected>1 hour</option><option value="PT2H">2 hours</option><option value="PT6H">6 hours</option><option value="PT12H">12 hours</option><option value="P1D">1 day</option></select><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long an access token is valid. Shorter = better security (revocation takes effect sooner) but more refresh round-trips. Default 1 hour is the common OAuth middle ground. A custom PHP <code>DateInterval</code> spec set in <code>tcms.php</code> (e.g. <code>PT45M</code>) is kept and shown as-is.</p></div><div class="form-field select-field " data-type="select" style="--grid-area: refreshTokenTtl;"><label for="field-UID">Refresh Token Lifetime</label><div class="form-group"><select id="field-UID" name="refreshTokenTtl" aria-describedby="help-UID"><option value="P1D">1 day</option><option value="P7D">7 days</option><option value="P14D">14 days</option><option value="P30D" selected>30 days</option><option value="P60D">60 days</option><option value="P90D">90 days</option><option value="P180D">180 days</option><option value="P1Y">1 year</option></select><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long a refresh token can be used to obtain new access tokens. After this, the user must re-authorize via the consent screen. Default 30 days. A custom PHP <code>DateInterval</code> spec set in <code>tcms.php</code> is kept and shown as-is.</p></div><div class="form-field select-field " data-type="select" style="--grid-area: authCodeTtl;"><label for="field-UID">Authorization Code Lifetime</label><div class="form-group"><select id="field-UID" name="authCodeTtl" aria-describedby="help-UID"><option value="PT1M">1 minute</option><option value="PT2M">2 minutes</option><option value="PT5M">5 minutes</option><option value="PT10M" selected>10 minutes</option></select><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long an authorization code is valid before it must be exchanged for tokens. Short by design — the user is mid-flow. RFC 6749 §4.1.2 recommends 10 minutes maximum. Default 10 minutes.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field " data-type="number" style="--grid-area: tokenEndpointLimit;"><label for="field-UID">Token Endpoint Rate Limit (per minute)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="tokenEndpointLimit" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="60" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="60" min="0" max="6000" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Maximum requests to <code>/oauth/token</code> per minute from a single IP. Protects against brute-force code exchange and runaway refresh loops. Applies to all callers (admin requests are NOT exempt). Set to <code>0</code> to disable. Default 60.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field  field-hidden" data-type="number" style="--grid-area: dynamicRegistrationLimit; display: none;" data-settings="{&quot;visibility&quot;:{&quot;watch&quot;:&quot;dynamicRegistration&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:true,&quot;operator&quot;:&quot;==&quot;}}"><label for="field-UID">Dynamic Registration Rate Limit (per hour)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="dynamicRegistrationLimit" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="60" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="60" min="0" max="1000" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Maximum requests to <code>/oauth/register</code> per hour from a single IP. Dynamic registration is an unauthenticated endpoint that creates persistent server state, so it is throttled — but hosted AI clients (Claude, ChatGPT) register from their provider's shared backend IPs and register again on every connect attempt, so too low a limit locks those connectors out for the rest of the hour. Default 60/hour. Set to <code>0</code> to disable.</p></div><div class="form-inline-fields"><button type="submit" class="button btn cms-save cms-button">Save Settings</button></div></form>